.TH CP2 1 "August 2026" "cp2 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cp2 \- rsync-style copy and sync over SSH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp2
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fISRC\fR \fIDST\fR
.br
.B cp2 \-\-server
.br
.B cp2 \-\-list-only \fISRC\fR \fIDST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cp2
is a pure-Rust, high-performance copy and sync tool for local directories
or over the network on Linux, macOS, and Windows. It copies files locally,
or syncs two machines over SSH with rsync-style semantics, using a
content-defined delta engine (FastCDC chunking + BLAKE3) that sends only
the bytes that actually changed. One binary, no runtime dependencies
beyond the
.B ssh
client you already have, and zero server setup on a same-platform remote
that runs an SSH server (sshd; the OpenSSH Server feature on Windows,
enabled once): the server binary is auto-deployed on first sync. A different platform
needs cp2 installed on the remote once — `cargo install cp2 --locked`, the
prebuilt release tarball (extract, rename the binary to `cp2`, copy it to
`~/.cargo/bin`), or a source build (`git clone` + `cargo build --release`).
The binary is `cp2` on Linux and macOS (no extension) and `cp2.exe` on
Windows, where the folder is `%USERPROFILE%\\.cargo\\bin`.
.PP
A sync deploys the server binary if needed, exchanges file manifests,
plans create/update/delete, and sends only the changed bytes \- a 1-byte
edit near the start of a 50 GB file transfers kilobytes, not gigabytes.
The quick check is size+mtime; \-\-checksum switches it to BLAKE3 hashes.
.SH TARGETS
A remote target is
.BI user@host : path
(ports are set with
\fB\-\-port\fR,
never parsed from the target string; a numeric suffix like
.B host:2222
is a path). The direction is inferred from which side is remote:
.B cp2 SRC user@host:DEST
pushes,
.B cp2 user@host:SRC DEST
pulls.
.PP
The remote
.I :path
follows rsync: a leading
.B /
(
.BI user@host :/abs/path
) is an absolute server path; anything else
(
.BI user@host :backup
) is relative to the serve root (the account home); no path means the
home itself. Traversal (
.B ..
) is rejected. The source
.I SRC
follows rsync's trailing-slash rule: with no trailing slash the last
component of the source is recreated under the destination.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p, \--port PORT\fP
SSH port (default 22). Ports are never parsed from the target string — a numeric suffix like \fBhost:2222\fP is a path
.TP
\fB\--jump-host USER@HOST[:PORT]\fP \fI(rsync ProxyJump)\fP
Tunnel through a jump host (russh transport, the Windows default; system ssh reads \fBProxyJump\fP from \fB~/.ssh/config\fP instead)
.TP
\fB\--password PASSWORD\fP
Supply the target ssh password directly instead of prompting. The russh transport (Windows) sends it once and scrubs it from memory immediately; the system-ssh transport (Unix) injects it natively into the master ssh spawn on a pty (the sshpass mechanism, no external tool; password auth is forced and the master uses a run-unique socket, so the injection always sees a prompt) and scrubs it right after. Visible in the process list and shell history while the run is active — prefer keys or the prompt
.TP
\fB\--jump-password PASSWORD\fP
Password for the jump host (\fB--jump-host\fP) when it differs from \fB--password\fP; without it the jump reuses \fB--password\fP (or the prompted value). Same visibility and scrubbing caveats
.TP
\fB\--password-file FILE\fP
Read the password from FILE (first line) instead of \fB--password\fP — only the file path rides the command line, so the secret never appears in the shell history or process list. Mutually exclusive with \fB--password\fP; keep the file \fBchmod 600\fP
.TP
\fB\--remote-path PATH\fP \fI(rsync \--rsync-path)\fP
Path of \fBcp2\fP on the remote, used as the remote command (default \fB~/.cargo/bin/cp2\fP; \fB%USERPROFILE%\\.cargo\\bin\\cp2.exe\fP on Windows). Shell-quoted on Unix remotes — spaces and shell metacharacters work, \fB~\fP still expands; on Windows remotes it is interpolated into a cmd/PowerShell command, so keep it free of spaces, quotes, and metacharacters
.TP
\fB\--binaries-dir DIR\fP
Directory holding prebuilt \fBcp2-<triple>\fP sidecars (checked before the directory next to this binary)
.TP
\fB\--no-auto-install\fP
Don't check/deploy the server binary before syncing
.TP
\fB\-a, \--archive\fP \fI(rsync \-a)\fP
Full archive (byte-identical): recursion + mode/mtime/symlinks/hard links (always on) plus special files (fifos, sockets, devices), owner/group (best-effort \fBchown\fP), SUID/SGID/Sticky, and \fB--literal-links\fP (literal link targets) — \fBUnix-like systems only\fP, silently skipped on Windows. Default (no \fB-a\fP) is 0-Root: permission bits only, owner is the SSH connection user
.TP
\fB\--literal-links\fP
Keep links and shortcuts as they are (rsync \fB-l\fP): every symlink is recreated with its \fBliteral target string\fP (no DEST-relative rewrite, no external-link dereference/skip), and Windows-source \fB.lnk\fP shortcuts are copied as opaque files. On a Windows target a symlink still materializes as a \fB.lnk\fP, but the target stays literal. Implied by \fB-a\fP; overridden by \fB--skip-links\fP/\fB--follow-links\fP
.TP
\fB\--literal-internal-links\fP
Keep \fIinternal\fP symlinks (targets resolving inside SRC) with their literal target instead of the DEST-relative rewrite; external links keep the default policy
.TP
\fB\--literal-external-file-links\fP
Keep \fIexternal file-target\fP symlinks as links with their literal absolute target instead of dereferencing them (high risk: the destination must have the same absolute path). Ignored when the destination is Windows
.TP
\fB\--literal-external-dir-links\fP
Keep \fIexternal directory-target\fP symlinks as links with their literal target instead of skipping them (high risk: the destination must have the same absolute path). Dangling external links are still skipped unless \fB--literal-links\fP
.TP
\fB\--follow-links\fP \fI(rsync \-L)\fP
Dereference every symlink (rsync \fB-L\fP): the target's content is copied in the link's place — file targets as regular files, directory targets recursed (with loop detection), Windows-source \fB.lnk\fP shortcuts followed to their target. Overrides the \fB--literal-*\fP family
.TP
\fB\--skip-links\fP \fI(rsync \--no-l)\fP
Skip every symlink and shortcut entirely: not synced, not followed (rsync \fB--no-l\fP). Highest priority — overrides all other link flags
.TP
\fB\--no-perms\fP \fI(rsync \--no-p)\fP
Don't preserve permission bits: the destination gets explicit 0644/0755 defaults (files/dirs) and the Windows-source executable heuristic is disabled
.TP
\fB\-S, \--sparse\fP \fI(rsync \-S)\fP
Write files sparsely on the receiver: runs of zeros ≥ 4096 bytes become holes instead of allocated blocks (VM images, database files). Content bytes are unchanged
.TP
\fB\-X, \--xattrs\fP \fI(rsync \-X)\fP
Copy extended attributes for files and directories (best-effort: unsettable names warn and skip; symlinks not covered). On Linux, POSIX ACLs ride along as \fBsystem.posix_acl_*\fP attributes
.TP
\fB\-U, \--atimes\fP \fI(rsync \-U)\fP
Restore the source's last-access time; without it the receiver's atime is left alone. Independent of \fB--no-times\fP; never part of the quick check
.TP
\fB\--no-times\fP \fI(rsync \--no-t)\fP
Don't preserve mtimes (destination gets transfer time; quick check becomes size-only)
.TP
\fB\--no-recursive\fP \fI(rsync \--no-r)\fP
Sync only the source root's direct files; skip subdirectories
.TP
\fB\-n, \--dry-run\fP \fI(rsync \-n)\fP
Print the transfer direction and exit without connecting (a local preview — rsync's \fB-n\fP runs a full remote dry scan with a per-file change list; cp2's \fB-n\fP is intentionally lighter)
.TP
\fB\-W, \--watch[=DUR]\fP
Watch SRC and sync changes continuously (push/local: event-driven; pull: server-driven); optional d/h/m/s duration (\fB-W=1h30m\fP), default 24h
.TP
\fB\--watch-delay MS\fP
Debounce quiet window for \fB--watch\fP (default 1000)
.TP
\fB\--delete\fP \fI(rsync \--delete)\fP
Remove destination files not present in source
.TP
\fB\--max-delete N\fP \fI(rsync \--max-delete)\fP
Refuse to delete more than N files with \fB--delete\fP
.TP
\fB\-u, \--update\fP \fI(rsync \-u)\fP
Skip files where destination is newer
.TP
\fB\-c, \--checksum\fP \fI(rsync \-c)\fP
Compare BLAKE3 hashes instead of size+mtime
.TP
\fB\--ignore-existing\fP \fI(rsync \--ignore-existing)\fP
Skip files that already exist
.TP
\fB\--existing\fP \fI(rsync \--existing)\fP
Only update existing files; don't create new ones
.TP
\fB\--ignore-times\fP \fI(rsync \-I)\fP
Transfer all files, ignoring the size+mtime quick check
.TP
\fB\--backup\fP \fI(rsync \--backup)\fP
Keep the replaced destination as \fB<name>~\fP
.TP
\fB\--remove-source-files\fP \fI(rsync \--remove-source-files)\fP
Delete source files only after the destination is \fBhash-verified and fsynced\fP (BLAKE3 compare, per-file fsync, size+mtime re-checks both sides; move-off workflows; directories and symlinks are kept)
.TP
\fB\--verify\fP
Verify the destination bytes match the source after the transfer (BLAKE3 on the fly, no re-reads; mismatches → exit 23, nothing deleted)
.TP
\fB\--files-from FILE\fP \fI(rsync \--files-from)\fP
Sync only the listed \fBabsolute\fP paths (one per line, Unix or Windows line endings), mirrored under DST; relative entries rejected; SRC not used (\fBcp2 --files-from FILE DST\fP). On a remote pull the entries are server-absolute paths. With \fB\--delete\fP the delete set is bounded by the listed paths (rsync scope): destination content outside them is left alone
.TP
\fB\--exclude GLOB\fP \fI(rsync \--exclude)\fP
Exclude matching paths (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\--include GLOB\fP \fI(rsync \--include)\fP
Include matching paths (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\--exclude-from FILE\fP \fI(rsync \--exclude-from)\fP
Read additional exclude patterns from FILE (one per line; blank lines and \fB#\fP/\fB;\fP comments ignored)
.TP
\fB\--include-from FILE\fP \fI(rsync \--include-from)\fP
Read additional include patterns from FILE (same format)
.TP
\fB\-i, \--itemize-changes\fP \fI(rsync \-i)\fP
Print a per-file change line: \fB>f\fP/\fBcd\fP new, \fB>f.s\fP updated, \fB*deleting\fP removed, \fB.f\fP in-sync (rsync \fB-i\fP; covers push/local out of the plan, pull from the reproduced plan). A chmod'ed in-sync file is re-applied as an attribute-only update and shows as \fB>f.s\fP with zero bytes
.TP
\fB\--stats\fP \fI(rsync \--stats)\fP
Print a detailed statistics block (files, bytes, time, skipped) after the summary
.TP
\fB\--list-only\fP \fI(rsync \--list-only)\fP
List the source files without transferring (local and remote sources)
.TP
\fB\-j, \--jobs N\fP
Parallel transfer + hash workers; omitted = auto-tuned from the target storage class
.TP
\fB\--storage auto|hdd|ssd\fP
Storage class for auto-tuning: detect on Linux/Windows/macOS (default), or force HDD/SSD
.TP
\fB\-z, \--compress\fP \fI(rsync \-z)\fP
Compress the data stream (lz4)
.TP
\fB\--bwlimit SIZE\fP \fI(rsync \--bwlimit)\fP
Limit transfer bandwidth (e.g. 10M, 100K)
.TP
\fB\--fsync\fP
fsync every received file before it is renamed into place, and the destination directories holding the renames once at the end (durable but slower; off by default)
.TP
\fB\-v, \--verbose\fP \fI(rsync \-v)\fP
Verbosity (repeat for more: -v, -vv, -vvv)
.TP
\fB\-q, \--quiet\fP \fI(rsync \-q)\fP
Suppress the non-error output: no per-file listing, no transfer summary, no deploy/watch lines (errors and the skipped-file report still print)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (usage, connection, protocol, deploy).
.TP
.B 23
Partial failure (rsync's convention): some files could not be
transferred \- \-\-verify reports a mismatch, or a file failed mid-run.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# First sync: pushes ./photos to ~/backup on the server, deploying cp2 there
cp2 ./photos user@server:backup

# Restore: pull it back down
cp2 user@server:backup ./restore

# Custom SSH port
cp2 \-p 2222 ./data user@host:backup

# Preview first, then sync
cp2 \-n ./data user@host:backup

# Move data off a capture instrument, then free its disk
cp2 \-\-verify \-\-remove-source-files /data user@host:storage

# Keep watching a folder for 12 hours
cp2 \-W=12h ./data user@host:backup

# Quoted glob sources are expanded by cp2
cp2 './src/*.rs' user@host:backup
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsync (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
The full manual, benchmark comparisons, and architecture notes live in the
repository README and ARCHITECTURE.
